  "response": "The question of whether poor neighborhoods harm their residents, or merely collect families who were already struggling, begins with a theoretical claim about how urban poverty works. William Julius Wilson argued that concentrated urban poverty isolates residents from jobs and mainstream institutions, a thesis documented in The Truly Disadvantaged, which Wilson published in 1997. If that isolation is real, it should leave a measurable mark on employment, health, and schooling. Observational comparisons, however, cannot separate the mechanism Wilson described from the way families sort themselves across the housing market. Households that end up in high-poverty tracts often differ in income history, health, and parental resources that surveys fail to capture. The outcome gap therefore has two candidate explanations, and at least one of them must hold, perhaps both: unobserved family characteristics account for part of the outcome gap between residents of poor and nonpoor neighborhoods, or neighborhood conditions causally produce part of that gap. Statistical controls for observed traits cannot rule out the first.\n\nSociologists and economists addressed this problem most directly through a federal housing demonstration. Random assignment was used in Moving to Opportunity to separate neighborhood effects from family self-selection. Because a lottery rather than parental choice decided which families received which offer, unmeasured traits such as motivation or resourcefulness should have been balanced across groups. The program operated in Baltimore, Boston, Chicago, Los Angeles, and New York. It assigned volunteer families to treatment groups by random lottery, and that assignment came after Moving to Opportunity began enrolling families in 1994. By contrast, Moving to Opportunity began enrolling families in 1984. The volunteers were overwhelmingly female-headed households living in public housing projects, the kind of setting that Wilson's argument treats as most isolating.\n\nThe value of the design depended on the conditions attached to the vouchers. Either the Moving to Opportunity experimental group was required to initially move to a census tract with a poverty rate below 10 percent, or it faced no restriction on the poverty rate of its initial destination tract; the two conditions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Whichever rule applied, the experiment's comparison of offered and non-offered families measures the effect of an opportunity to relocate, not the effect of any particular address. Many families who received offers never used them, and many who did move later returned to poorer areas. Researchers predicted that moving to low-poverty areas would raise the employment of Moving to Opportunity adults. That forecast resulted from the conclusion of the final Moving to Opportunity evaluation that the voucher offer did not significantly raise adult employment or earnings. For a demonstration built partly on Wilson's emphasis on job access, this labor-market result was the most consequential finding for the theory that motivated it.\n\nThe evaluation's other findings complicate any simple verdict on neighborhood effects. Despite the absence of labor-market gains, adults offered low-poverty vouchers in Moving to Opportunity showed lower rates of extreme obesity and diabetes. The authors of the final evaluation determined that these two findings do not conflict, concluding that the voucher offer improved adult well-being through channels other than the labor market. These health improvements are consistent with reduced stress, greater perceived safety, and better access to food and recreation. By contrast, children who moved to low-poverty areas before age 13 through Moving to Opportunity earned more as adults than control-group children. In other words, control-group children in Moving to Opportunity earned less as adults than children who moved to low-poverty areas before age 13. This age gradient matters for interpretation. Children who relocated as adolescents showed no comparable gains, which suggests that the effect of a neighborhood accumulates with the number of years a child spends in it, rather than appearing as soon as a family arrives.\n\nTaken together, these results show why the debate over selection and causation has outlived its original framing. A lottery removes self-selection from the comparison it creates, but it cannot make every outcome respond to that comparison. Adult earnings proved largely unresponsive to relocation, whereas adult health and children's later earnings did respond. The experimental record therefore supports a narrower claim than either early structural accounts or skeptical selection accounts proposed. Neighborhood effects appear to be real but selective. Their size depends on which outcome is measured and on the age at which residents are exposed.",
